today we are heading off planet earth  and going to the moon  the moon is back in vogue again whether  it's the united states or china or  billionaires  lots of people seem to want to go there  and we're going to talk  to a person named jessie kate shingler  she's worked at nasa ames she's worked  at a rocket company  and she is part of an organization  called open lunar  it's a non-profit and they're doing  something  that's really interesting rethinking  different ways that we  might govern and set up settlements  on the moon and maybe find a way to  do things better and at least  differently on the moon than we have  on on earth with that we are going to go  to france  where jessie kate is spending a little  covered time  [Music]  well thanks for doing this with us can  you for people who don't know  about the open lunar foundation can you  tell us what  the mission is yeah open lunar is  interested in seeing a peaceful and  cooperative  sustained human presence on the moon  and ensuring that that happens in a way  that  is open and accessible and uh  you know as as soon as possible i'm not  sure that unless you're if you're just a  casual  observer of all this stuff that people  are fully aware of how much  reinvigorated interest there is in the  moon right now  tell me you know if we think globally  what's going on between china and the us  and various factions trying to try to  get to the moon in the next few years  the united states uh has been and  continues to be  one of the major players in the space  industry  it has a program called the artemis  program that is focused on getting  humans back to the moon  for the first time since the apollo  program during the apollo program  russia of course was the the major other  player in the space  world uh and that introduced a dynamic  that was you know a cold war dynamic  where russia  and the united states were competing for  uh getting to the moon first  today what we see is something that has  some analogs to that between  the united states and china who is the  other major state  actor in the space world china also has  plans to go to the lunar surface with  humans  with taikonauts and both united states  and china have plans to set up a  base of some kind or an outpost on the  lunar surface  but of course they're not the only ones  interested in going to the lunar surface  there are a lot of other countries who  are developing capabilities either to  collaborate with those countries  or to send their own missions and then  there are private actors who are  planning to go to the lunar surface  of course everybody's familiar with  spacex and blue origin at this point  and they're companies that have  leadership with a very strong vision for  seeing a sustained  human presence on the lunar surface and  then i mean like if you go back 10 years  ago  right when spacex was was kind of  starting to hit its stride and  and bezos was putting a lot of money  into blue origin i mean it seemed like  mars  you know elon's very focused on mars and  mars was like the  the talk and obviously there's things  you can do there that you can't do on  the moon but it's kind of funny i mean  the moon like  just sort of came back in vogue very  quickly it felt like  to me i mean you know what do you why do  you think  that is if you think if if you do think  that's what actually kind of happened  yeah i mean um you know in the space  community of course we love to debate  moon versus mars  and uh and it's fun and they're they're  very different places  and there are a lot of reasons for us to  go to mars but the moon  is uh much closer and it's somewhere  that we can  go and sort of learn the ropes of uh  having human presence and extended human  presence in outer space  we can get our transportation systems in  order we can get our human habitation  systems and order our communication  systems in order so you know i think  most folks with a kind of long-term  vision  for space exploration and settlement in  general  would see them not as being in  competition with one another but  but the moon is being a stepping stone  uh to mars and other kinds of activities  uh and just building on our presence on  the moon hope you know hopefully what  we'll see  is uh the development of sustained human  presence on the moon and going to mars  and i wanted to i wanted to get into a  little bit about  why you're so into the moon and in space  in general you have this you have a  pretty interesting history when it comes  to  all this tell me you know tell me a  little bit about how you got into all  this  yeah so uh i guess i'll say that that i  actually wasn't your typical kid who  was into space i never had this sort of  grand vision of  being an astronaut i wasn't a nerd about  rockets  and i actually ended up by accident in  a meeting of folks who were  working on sort of space advocacy and  as a result of this meeting i ended up  attending this conference  at the united nations where a number of  young people  were present and um one of the outcomes  of it was that we started  actually a young people sort of uh group  that was called the space generation  advisory council and that  group ended up having observer status at  the united nations  and sort of bringing together the  perspectives of young people from around  the world  to provide input to the un's process on  um  on thinking about the role of space and  how it can help humanity  so this group of friends that you made  during this conference  a lot of you stay as very close friends  and in fact i mean you  this is like what it's like 20 years  later i mean you guys a big chunk of you  guys like still live together right  basically you know we lived together in  this hotel  for it was only two weeks but of course  when you're 18 or 20 or whatever  it was this extremely formative  experience and it just so happened that  you know a year or two later  enough of us ended up actually living in  dc for a while  that we ended up getting a house  together and so we would kind of  host folks when they came to town so we  had people staying with us  and then being inspired from some of the  ideas about what the possibilities for  society  can be and thinking about how could we  kind of embody some of those changes  and sort of test them out in in um in  small ways even just in our home  and so you know over time we've did that  we have lived together in  you know in different cities and and i  really are you know like family at this  point  [Music]  in san francisco there's a number of  houses that you've been not just where  you live but you've been influential in  starting the places like the rainbow  mansion and the embassy they're known as  these kind of communal houses these  intentional communities where  everybody's sharing in the governance of  the house and how it operates and  it's living this life for the past 20 or  so  years that has in some ways shaped your  thinking  about seeing the moon maybe as a place  where we could  have a bit of a reset and and rethink  the way  we might build this next community off  of our  our planet is that fair i think i'll say  yes and no  in my opinion what we want to do is  learn from  what we've done on this planet and think  about how we can incorporate that  knowledge in order to do things  uh you know bigger and better um  as we move forward both of course here  uh as well as on the moon  i uh i also think that the moon because  of the outer space treaty as the  predominant  governing agreement for the outer space  domain because that treaty contains  provisions that require us to make space  accessible to  everyone and not to appropriate  territory in outer space  there is a lot in common that the moon  has and  our governance of the moon will have for  challenges like climate change  here on earth and so in that sense i  think it's it's in particular something  that we really need to think about  um extending our our lessons learned  here but also looking at where we have  failed to learn lessons here  and use it to prototype new approaches  throughout all this history right it's  been like the  who's going to get to the moon first the  u.s or russia and then and then it's  been this very  nation-driven thing and i know you know  you guys wanted to see  something more of bringing a lot of  countries together bringing a lot of  different  parties to stop treating this like some  place to be  conquered and divvied up and more like  how can we kind of do this together can  we come with some open standards  i remember some of the discussions it's  just interesting that like it would come  down to even like what type of  plug are we going to have on the lunar  base is it going to be like you know  some u.s standard or european standard i  mean tell me about  you know what you would like to see  what's your vision  your ideal vision for as we start to go  to the moon and build settlements what  that should look like  yeah so i think the example you gave of  standards is a really interesting one  because if you look even for example to  the international space station which i  think is one of  you know the most successful examples of  international collaboration  uh and you still have outlets that  are different in the russian section as  in the american section  so you know getting together and  thinking about  what are the things that we want to  share in in  these sort of new environments and what  are what are the things that actually  can be different  uh i think is also a really important  question okay let's  let's just assume that both china in the  u.s are going  to the moon and possibly trying to set  up settlements  how do you expect this to play out and  what do you see  open lunar's role being in this future  it seems quite likely that what we will  see is  in the next say 10 or 15 years at least  two different outposts on the lunar  surface run by two different  governments from terrestrial nation  states  and and so i think that's going to be  really interesting and that's when we're  going to start to really see the test of  what does it mean  to not be able to exclude other actors  from  you know any particular region of the  surface of the moon  and that to me is is where organizations  like openlunar  can help out because we're asking what  are a number of different ways that we  could approach  thinking about for example property  rights and resource management  jeff bezos has talked a lot about  wanting to set up factories in space and  industrializing space elon's talked a  lot about  different ways to set up way stations  either in orbit or maybe something on  the moon to like help  get to mars more easily like you know i  mean we are kind of like at this point  where  where it might be one of these  billionaires that's like harvesting  something on the moon before  a country because these guys especially  elon move really fast  um yeah so you know what what do you see  as the role of the individual or the  corporation  in all this yeah well i mean i think  one approach would be to say we should  slow down so that we can think about  this more carefully  but i also think that there's a risk  there that if  governments slow down to think about it  more carefully that actually the private  sector will just get there first  and we'll get there with even less  guidance and uh the private actors that  are going to the moon or you know  anywhere in space  those activities uh are being approved  by state governments  um but what happens when you're sort of  outside the eyes and ears  of those governments once you're on the  surface of another planet you know i  think  um nobody knows at this point and it's  going to be important that we choose a  minimal set of things that we  want to have consensus on as an  international community so for example  when we talk about resources on on the  surface of any planetary body  i don't think they're all going to be  governed in the same way  some resources are scarce some are not  so in space  if we set up specific regimes that  respond to the specific nature of  different kinds of resources  uh we will get better input from  different stakeholders  uh that will make our our  decision-making processes more effective  and certain if people want to get like a  better sense of  dig in deeper to kind of you know what  open lunar's about and what you've been  thinking about i know you just gave a  ted talk recently and you guys have  you've got a website are those kind of  like the best places for people to go  and what's on tap for you guys next year  you know as you pursue this  so uh pretty much everything is on our  website  and for our work in the next year or two  i think  in my opinion what we want to do is have  more consultation  in the space community from private  actors from civil society  and we've been just starting to convene  conversations about near-term policy  issues  uh for lunar activity that can inform  what's going to be happening in the moon  and  sort of five to ten year time frame the  last question i guess  it's inevitable in many ways uh maybe  more realistic than it would have been  10 years ago or so are you gonna take a  trip you're gonna i would you would you  be like an active member of a lunar  settlement  oh yeah of course i would  i mean the way i see that playing out  right is that i think we're gonna start  to see  like longer and longer stays on the moon  you know  weeks and then months and then years and  that that process of starting to figure  out  what decisions are made by sort of like  console operators back on earth  versus by those who are kind of on the  surface themselves and how that relates  to the resources they have and  and a sense of kind of um fairness and  justice  is going to be really fascinating
 

